{"answer":"Scikit‑learn ships cross‑validation helpers (e.g. `cross_val_score`, `cross_val_predict`, `learning_curve`, etc.) so users don’t have to write boilerplate code for splitting the data, fitting a model on each split, collecting predictions, evaluating a score, and aggregating the results. The utilities:

• provide a consistent API across estimators and pipelines;
• encapsulate common best‑practice workflow (cloning, parallel execution, reproducibility, proper handling of weights and scoring functions);
• expose a variety of ready‑made CV strategies (`KFold`, `StratifiedKFold`, `ShuffleSplit`, `LeaveOneOut`, etc.) via a single `check_cv` helper;
• support convenient collective metrics (mean, std, percentile) and optional ranking of folds; and
• integrate seamlessly into scikit‑learn’s model‑selection, hyper‑parameter search, and calibration pipelines.

By bundling these utilities, scikit‑learn reduces the potential for user error, promotes reproducible modeling, and makes it easier to start with common tasks without implementing cross‑validation logic from scratch."}

